Trump's post on Truth Social
Thursday, 22 January 2026, 1:01 PM
by Jacob Goodpipe
Visible to participants on this course

Background:

The image Donald Trump posted to the Truth social media platform features himself presenting a map with Canada, Greenland, and Venezuela covered with American flags. The other people featured in the photo include leaders from other countries, including Prime Minister (PM) Keir Starmer from the U.K., PM Giorgia Meloni from Italy, the German Chancellor Friedrich Merz, President of France Emmanuel Macron, President of Finland Alexander Stubb, President of Ukraine Volodymyr Zelenskyy, and the NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte. (source)

The map displayed in the image was altered using AI. The original photo came from August 18, 2025, when European leaders met in the Whitehouse to discuss the security of Ukraine, and the map in the photo was a map of Russian-occupied territories in Ukraine. You can view the original image here, on the official Whitehouse government site.
Is the distinction that the image is not real important?

Although the contents of an AI generated image may not be real, the motivation and intention behind the image still are. Given that this was posted by someone with authority such as the President of the United States, it can be considered a threat towards Canada and Greenland. Additionally, with the United States' recent military action in Venezuela (source) and the fact that Canada and Greenland are grouped with Venezuela in the image, the threat becomes more legitimate.

Similar to our discussion about GROK AI's ban and consent, the image posted by Donald Trump implicates that European leaders and NATO are involved in this discussion without their consent. Canada is a founding member of NATO, and with the variety of world leaders featured in the photo, the statement made by posting this photo threatens the treaty.
Are threatening, trolling, etc. posts tolerated or welcomed on social media platforms?

Trump was banned from Twitter in 2021 after he spurred on the storming of the Capitol on January 6th, showing that mainstream social media platforms will not tolerate threats. Truth Social, however, is owned by the Trump Media & Technology Group, meaning Trump has the authority to post whatever he would like. (https://www.britannica.com/topic/Truth-Social)

For social media platforms, the extent of what is allowed is dependent on the rules set out by the platform. This leaves a wide range of tolerance towards trolling and threatening posts, with most platforms leaning towards lenient policies that seem to welcome trolling, as they benefit off the "rage economy" discussed last class, and the notable cost in labour and user experience to enforce policy moderation. Most platforms end up being moderated by the users, meaning as long as the post is aligned with the prevailing ideology within the community it is posted in, it is accepted.
Is that style of communication effective?

The effectiveness of that style of communication depends on what your goal is. If the objective of the communication is to encourage intolerance and keep people engaged with the platform, then it is a very effective method. However, if the objective is to facilitate productive discussion and create a better society, then it is ineffective as it discourages spaces for civil discussion and increases political/ideological division within society.


In the current digital landscape, social media platforms profit from user's engagement and attention, and regulation only goes against their economic interests. In this model, companies will only change if external pressures are applied, most likely from government. Our group still has many questions about what the future of social media platforms could look like. Can governments impose policies on social media platforms without suppressing free speech? What would policies that improve these platforms look like? And can AI play a part in bettering these online spaces?

