The Risk and Rewards of Generative AIs or LLMs
Friday, 16 January 2026, 5:12 PM
by Elior Kerimov
Visible to participants on this course
Grok has been banned in several countries (such as Malaysia and Indonesia) due to its ability and misuse to create non-consensual generative images of people. I want to discuss how Grok connects to the seven koans of bits, specifically "Koan 1: it's all just bits". Grok clearly illustrates that it does not know consent, people, or dignity, it only sees bits representing pixels, prompts, etc. A real person's likeness is essentially just reduced to data that Grok or other LLMs process and train on. Laws and ethics distinguishes real photos, edited images, and deepfakes. However, Grok does not distinguish between them meaningfully, unless explicitly constrained. Grok treats people, their likeness, and identity as data, not as personhood. 

The existence of Grok and other AIs bring about a multitude of legal and ethical issues. However, it can be argued that these issues can be alleviated by constraining what the AIs can process and generate, but I believe the risks still outweigh the rewards. The reward in this context is the ability to create generative images. The risks are but not exclusively the legal, ethical, privacy, and copyright issues that arise from creating generative AI. Moreover, the amount of power and databases needed to keep running these AIs and the data they train on is devastating on the climate and the cost of running them ultimately falls on the regular taxpayer (i.e. increase in payments for electrical utilities and subsidies funded by the taxpayer). Furthermore, once non-consensual images have been uploaded/generated it forever stays there forever, haunting the victims until the end of days.

All of these risks massively outweigh the reward of having the ability to make generative images, that were most likely trained on copyrighted data (this is a whole other issue that I will not go into). The devastation of the planet, the financial burden on the taxpayer, and the legal, ethical, privacy, and copyright issues, massively outweigh the ability of AIs to make generative images that essentially provides zero benefit/productivity to society.
