by Ikechukwu Nwigwe
Visible to participants on this course

Privacy is the right of individuals to keep their personal information protected and to control who has access to it. It is important because the world contains harmful people such as traffickers, criminals, and pedophiles. When personal information is collected and sold, it creates serious risks, as individuals can be targeted, tracked, or exploited based on that data.

One major issue with collecting information about users is that it invades privacy. User data is often collected by companies and apps that involve many employees or third parties whom users do not know or trust personally. Although there are laws and company policies meant to protect user data, these protections do not always work. We frequently hear about data leaks, leaked court files, and stolen documents on social media and in the news. In many cases, these leaks are caused by people working inside the companies themselves. When such breaches occur, it is often difficult or impossible to identify the exact individual or group responsible, especially when large companies are involved. This lack of accountability increases the risk to users. For these reasons, privacy is important because once personal information is exposed, users lose control over how it is used and who can access it.


