Our group's discussion regarding Malaysia and Indonesia's decision to block the xAI tool, Grok, brought forth many questions about the ethics of AI tools, as well as the enforcement of AI restrictions.

One of the most visible risks of AI is the ability to generate fake content, and this is an excellent example of this. In relation to Koan 1 - "It's all just bits" - although the explicit images generated are "just bits" and are not real, they can easily be mistaken for real photos. AI generated images are becoming more realistic by the day, and these images can have very real impacts on people's lives.

AI does have its benefits - it increases productivity, can make information and data more approachable, and can enhance the experience of menial tasks. With AI image generation, it is unclear how the risks of misinformation, invasion of personal privacy, and mental health impacts outweigh the benefits in the current state of AI.

We also discussed the impacts of Malaysia and Indonesia's blocking of Grok. The block will prevent some users from accessing Grok, however, more tech-savvy users will be able to bypass this block. If the average user in those countries believes the tool is blocked, then inappropriate images generated by users who bypass it may be harder to prove as AI generated, since most people will believe that the tool cannot be accessed.

The risks of the xAI tool, Grok, seem to outweigh the benefits. As Koan 6 says, "Nothing goes away," and the images generated by these AI tools cannot be destroyed once they are generated. This story is a reminder for us to consider the ramifications of AI usage, both as users of the tools and as future developers of them.

